	<exercises>
    	<title>Practice Problems</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>For all questions, assume that the supply pins are connected to a suitable source that will not cause the output to saturate.</p>
		</introduction>

    	<subexercises>
			<title>Divided Feedback Op-Amp Circuits</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the output voltage and circuit gain of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit41" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit41">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit41">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with a 0.5V source, 1k resistor, and 6k resistor. The circuit has 4 nodes. The high-potential end of the 0.5V source and 1k resistor connect at node A. The 1k resistor, 6k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node B. The 6k resistor and output of the op-amp connect at node C, labeled Vout. The low-potential end of the 0.5V source and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to GND at node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=0.5~V] ($(opamp.-)-(2,1.5)$) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp.out)+(0,0)$);		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= -3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>G \amp= -6</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The circuit is an inverting op-amp circuit. Apply <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate the gain.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G \amp= -\frac{6~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Apply <xref ref="ch4-effectivegainopamp" /> to calculate the output voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= GV_{IN}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (-6)(0.5~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the output voltage and circuit gain of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit42" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit42">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit42">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with one 500mA source, 10ohm resistor, 50k resistor, and 30k resistor. The circuit has 4 nodes. The 500mA source, 10ohm resistor, and non-inverting input connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The 50k resistor, 30k resistor, and inverting input connect at node B. The 30k resistor connects to the output of the op-amp at node C, labeled Vout. The 50k resistor, 10ohm resistor, and current source connect to GND at node D. The current source flows out of node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short]						++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [short]							($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-]			++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,2.5)$) to [I, l^=500~mA]			++(up:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,2.5)$) to [short]					++(right:2);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] 						($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$]				($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=30~k$\Omega$]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*]			($(opamp.out)+(0,0)$);		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>G \amp= 1.6</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The circuit is a non-inverting op-amp circuit. Apply <xref ref="ch4-noninvertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate the gain.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G \amp= 1 + \frac{30~\textrm{k}\Omega}{50~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.6</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The voltage at the non-inverting node must be calculated before using <xref ref="ch4-effectivegainopamp" /> to calculate the output voltage. The voltage at the non-inverting node is equal to the voltage dropped over the 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, which can be calculated with Ohm's law. (To obtain a unit of V, first convert the source current to A to multiply with &#8486;. Alternatively, convert the resistance to k&#8486; to multiply with mA.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_P \amp= (10~\Omega)(0.5~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now use <xref ref="ch4-effectivegainopamp" /> to calculate the output voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= GV_P</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1.6)(5~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the output voltage and source gains of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit43" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit43">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit43">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 10mV (V1) source, 300mV (V2) source, 2k resistor, 5k resistor, and 10k resistor. The high-potential end of V1 connects to the 2k resistor at node A. The high-potential end of V2 connects to the 5k resistor at node B. The 2k resistor, 5k resistor, 10k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node C. The 10k resistor connects to the output of the op-amp at node D. The low-potential end of both sources and non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to GND at node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_={10~mV~(V1)}] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] ++(right:2);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(0.5,2)$) to [V, l_={300~mV~(V2)}] ++(down:1.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-] ++(down:2);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short] ++(left:0.5);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp.out)+(0,0)$);		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= -0.65~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>G_1 \amp= -5</mrow>
							<mrow>G_2 \amp= -2</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The circuit is an inverting summing amplifier circuit. Apply <xref ref="ch4-invertingsummingampeq" /> to calculate the output voltage and each of the source gains. The unit of each source has been converted to V.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= -\left(\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)0.01~\textrm{V}-\left(\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)0.3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.05~\textrm{V} - 0.6~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.65~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>G_1 \amp= -\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -5</mrow>
							<mrow>G_2 \amp= -\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the circuit gain of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit44" /> as a function of <m>x</m>. The potentiometer has a total resistance of 500<nbsp />&#8486;. Calculate the minimum and maximum possible values of the circuit gain. Use the variable <m>x</m> to describe the potentiometer resistance between the wiper and 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit44">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit44">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with voltage source Vin, 100ohm resistor, and 500ohm potentiometer. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node A. The 100ohm resistor and 500ohm pot connect at node B. The wiper of the 500ohm pot connects to the inverting input of the op-amp at node C. The 500ohm pot connects to the op-amp output at node D, labeled Vout. The low-potential end of Vin and the 100ohm resistor connect to GND at node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=$V_{IN}$] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(-1,1.25)$) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$] ++(left:2) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(1,1.25)$) to [pR, l_=500~$\Omega$, name=pot] ($(opamp.-)+(-1,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short] (pot.wiper);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++(up:0.5) |- ($(opamp.-)+(1,1.25)$);		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G \amp= 1 + \frac{500~\Omega - x}{100~\Omega + x}</mrow>
							<mrow>G_{min} \amp= 1</mrow>
							<mrow>G_{max} \amp= 6</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The resistance of the potentiometer is 500<nbsp />&#8486;. This means that the sum of the input resistance (resistance between the wiper and the 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, which the question states to express as the variable <m>x</m>) and feedback resistance (resistance between the wiper and output of the op-amp) is equal to 500<nbsp />&#8486;.</p>

					<p>The circuit is a non-inverting op-amp circuit. Apply <xref ref="ch4-noninvertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate the gain. The <m>R_1</m> value in <xref ref="ch4-noninvertingopampgaineq" /> refers to the resistance between the inverting node and ground, which is equal to <m>x + 100~\Omega</m>. The <m>R_2</m> value in <xref ref="ch4-noninvertingopampgaineq" /> refers to the feedback resistance (the resistance between the inverting node and the output), which is equal to <m>500~\Omega - x</m>.</p>

					<p>The gain of the circuit can now be calculated.</p>

					<p>
						<md>G = 1 + \frac{500~\Omega - x}{100~\Omega + x}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The smallest possible circuit gain occurs when the feedback resistance is 0<nbsp />&#8486; and the input resistance is 500<nbsp />&#8486;. In other words, when <m>x = 500~\Omega</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G_{min} \amp = 1 + \frac{500~\Omega - 500~\Omega}{100~\Omega + 500~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The largest possible circuit gain occurs when the feedback resistance is 500<nbsp />&#8486; and the input resistance is 0<nbsp />&#8486;. In other words, when <m>x = 0~\Omega</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G_{min} \amp = 1 + \frac{500~\Omega - 0~\Omega}{100~\Omega + 0~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1 + 5</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the output voltage and source gains of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit45" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit45">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit45">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 3V source (V1), 5V source (V2), 1k resistor, 5k resistor, 10k resistor, and 2k resistor. The high-potential end of V1 connects to the 1k resistor at node A. The 1k resistor, 2k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node B. The 2k resistor connects to the output of the op-amp at node C, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of V2 connects to the 5k resistor at node D. The 5k resistor, 10k resistor, and non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node E. The low-potential end of both sources and the 10k resistor connect to GND at node F.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=5~V~(V2)] ($(opamp.+)-(2,1.5)$) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$] (opamp.+);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, *-] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.25,0)$) to [V, l_=3~V~(V1)] ($(opamp.-)-(4.25,1.5)$) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.25,0)$) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp.out)+(0,0)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>G_1 \amp= -2</mrow>
							<mrow>G_2 \amp= 2</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The circuit is a difference amplifier. To calculate the output voltage, apply <xref ref="ch4-differenceopamp" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= -\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}3~\textrm{V} + \left(\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6~\textrm{V} + (3)(0.67)(5~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6~\textrm{V} + 10~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Apply <xref ref="ch4-difference-v1gain" /> to calculate <m>G_1</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G_1 \amp= -\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Apply <xref ref="ch4-difference-v2gain" /> to calculate <m>G_2</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G_2 \amp= \left(\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (3)(0.67)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex6">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the output voltage and circuit gain of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit46" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit46">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex6" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit46">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 0.5V source, 1k resistor, 2k-a resistor, 5k resistor, 2k-b resistor, and 4k resistor. The circuit has 5 nodes. The 1k and 2k-a resistor connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node A. The 2k-a resistor, 4k resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node B, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of the 0.5V source connects to the 5k resistor at node C. The 5k resistor, 2k-b resistor, 4k resistor, and non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node D. The low-potential end of the 0.5V source, 1k resistor, and 2k-b resistor connect to GND at node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++ (right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=0.5~V] ($(opamp.+)-(2,1.5)$) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$] (opamp.+);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [short, *-*] ++(down:1);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,1)$) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$] ++(down:2) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,1)$) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$] ++(right:2.75);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(-2.75,1)$) to [short, -*]	($(opamp.out)+(0.37,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(3.5,0)$) node[ground] {} to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp.out)+(0,0)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= 1.5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>G \amp= 3</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>This circuit doesn't fit any of the standard op-amp circuit templates (inverting, non-inverting, summing, or difference amplifier). Therefore, the assumptions of an op-amp circuit (no input current, virtual node) can be applied with any useful circuit laws and theorems to calculate these parameters.</p>

					<p>Apply KCL at the inverting input. The voltage at the inverting input will be referred to as <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{0-V_X}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X-V_{OUT}}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>-V_X \amp= 0.5V_X - 0.5V_{OUT}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 1.5V_X - 0.5V_{OUT}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Apply KCL at the non-inverting input. The voltage at the non-inverting will be equal to <m>V_X</m> due to the virtual node.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{0.5~\textrm{V} - V_X}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{V_X-V_{OUT}}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.1~\textrm{V} - 0.2V_X \amp= 0.5V_X + 0.25V_X - 0.25V_{OUT}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.1~\textrm{V} \amp= 0.95V_X - 0.25V_{OUT}</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>V_{OUT}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1.5 	\amp -0.5 	\amp 0\\
								0.95 	\amp -0.25 	\amp 0.1\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.5\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp 1.5\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{OUT}</m> is therefore equal to 1.5<nbsp />V. Use <xref ref="ch4-effectivegainopamp" /> to calculate the circuit gain.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G \amp= \frac{1.5~\textrm{V}}{0.5~\textrm{V}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex7">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the output voltage and source gains of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit47" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit47">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex7" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit47">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 4V source (V1), 25mA source (I2), 200ohm resistor, 500ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 1k resistor. The current source, 200ohm resistor, 500ohm resistor, and 600ohm resistor connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The 500ohm resistor, 1k resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node B, labeled Vout. The 600ohm resistor, 1k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node C. The high-potential end of V1 connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node D. The current source, 200ohm resistor, and low-potential end of V1 connect to GND at node E. The current source flows out of node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=4~V~(V1)] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(up:2);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++(up:0.5) |- ($(opamp.-)+(0,2)$);		
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(-2.25,2)$) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] ++(right:2.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.25,0)$) to [R, l^=600~$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.25,0)$) to [short] ++(up:2);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.25,0)$) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:2) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.25,2)$) to [I, l^=25~mA~(I2)] ++(up:2) --++ (right:2);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.25,2)$) --++ (right:2);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= 3.31~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>G_1 \amp= 1.69</mrow>
							<mrow>G_2 \amp= -0.69</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Perform a source transformation on the current source to obtain a circuit with two voltage sources. The feedback resistors have also been re-drawn in a manner that might make the circuit analysis more intuitive to solve.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit47sol1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram after source transformation.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit47sol1">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 4V source (V1), 5V source (V2), 200ohm resistor, 500ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 1k resistor. The circuit has 6 nodes. The high-potential end of V2 connects to the 200ohm resistor at node A. The 200ohm resistor, 500ohm resistor, and 600ohm resistor connect at node B. The 600ohm resistor, 1k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node C. The 500ohm resistor, 1k resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node D, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of V2 connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node E. The low-potential end of both sources connect to GND at node F.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=4~V~(V1)] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, -*] ++(right:2.38);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++(up:0.5) |- ($(opamp.-)+(0,2)$);		
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(-2.25,2)$) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] ++(right:2.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.25,0)$) to [R, l^=600~$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.25,0)$) to [short] ++(up:2);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.25,0)$) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$, -*] ++(right:2);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.25,0)$) to [V, l_=5~V~(V2)] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit doesn't fit any of the standard op-amp circuit templates (inverting, non-inverting, summing, or difference amplifier). Therefore, the assumptions of an op-amp circuit (no input current, virtual node) can be applied with any useful circuit laws and theorems to calculate these parameters.</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp. The voltage at the node at the intersection between the 200<nbsp />&#8486;, 500<nbsp />&#8486;, and 600<nbsp />&#8486; resistors will be referred to as <m>V_X</m>. All resistor units will be expressed in k&#8486;.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_X - 4~\textrm{V}}{0.6~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{4~\textrm{V} - V_{OUT}}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>1.67V_X - 6.67~\textrm{V} \amp= 4~\textrm{V} - V_{OUT}</mrow>
							<mrow>1.67V_X + V_{OUT} \amp= 10.67~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node at the intersection between the 200<nbsp />&#8486;, 500<nbsp />&#8486;, and 600<nbsp />&#8486; resistors.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{5~\textrm{V}-V_X}{0.2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X - 4~\textrm{V}}{0.6~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{V_X - V_{OUT}}{0.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>25~\textrm{V} - 5V_X \amp= 1.67V_X - 6.67~\textrm{V} + 2V_X - 2V_{OUT}</mrow>
							<mrow>8.67V_X - 2V_{OUT} \amp= 31.67~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>V_{OUT}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1.67 	\amp 1 	\amp 10.67\\
								8.67 	\amp -2 \amp 31.67\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 4.42\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp 3.31\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{OUT}</m> is therefore equal to 3.31<nbsp />V.</p>

					<p>Deactivate <m>V_2</m> to calculate the output voltage contribution from <m>V_1</m> to calculate <m>G_1</m>.</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_X - 4~\textrm{V}}{0.6~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{4~\textrm{V} - V_{OUT,1}}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>1.67V_X - 6.67~\textrm{V} \amp= 4~\textrm{V} - V_{OUT,1}</mrow>
							<mrow>1.67V_X + V_{OUT,1} \amp= 10.67~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node at the intersection between the 200<nbsp />&#8486;, 500<nbsp />&#8486;, and 600<nbsp />&#8486; resistors.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{-V_X}{0.2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X - 4~\textrm{V}}{0.6~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{V_X - V_{OUT,1}}{0.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>- 5V_X \amp= 1.67V_X - 6.67~\textrm{V} + 2V_X - 2V_{OUT,1}</mrow>
							<mrow>8.67V_X - 2V_{OUT,1} \amp= 6.67~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>V_{OUT,1}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1.67 	\amp 1 	\amp 10.67\\
								8.67 	\amp -2 \amp 6.67\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 2.33\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp 6.77\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{OUT,1}</m> is therefore equal to 6.77<nbsp />V. The source gain from source <m>V_1</m> can now be calculated.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G_1 \amp= \frac{6.77~\textrm{V}}{4~\textrm{V}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.69</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Deactivate <m>V_1</m> to calculate the output voltage contribution from <m>V_2</m> to calculate <m>G_2</m>.</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_X}{0.6~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{-V_{OUT,2}}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>1.67V_X \amp= - V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
							<mrow>1.67V_X + V_{OUT,2} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node at the intersection between the 200<nbsp />&#8486;, 500<nbsp />&#8486;, and 600<nbsp />&#8486; resistors.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{5~\textrm{V}-V_X}{0.2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X}{0.6~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{V_X - V_{OUT,2}}{0.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>25~\textrm{V} - 5V_X \amp= 1.67V_X + 2V_X - 2V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
							<mrow>8.67V_X - 2V_{OUT,2} \amp= 25~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>V_{OUT,2}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1.67 	\amp 1 	\amp 0\\
								8.67 	\amp -2 \amp 25\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 2.33\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp -3.47\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{OUT,2}</m> is therefore equal to <minus />3.47<nbsp />V. The source gain from source <m>V_2</m> can now be calculated.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G_2 \amp= \frac{-3.47~\textrm{V}}{5~\textrm{V}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.69</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex8">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the value of <m>R_{LOAD}</m> that should be used in <xref ref="examplecircuit48" /> to maximize the power transferred to that load resistor. Then, calculate the power consumed by <m>R_{LOAD}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit48">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex8" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit48">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit containing a 9V source, 5k resistor, 8k resistor, 10k resistor, 20k resistor, 4k resistor, and Rload. The circuit has 6 nodes. The 5k resistor, 20k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node A. The 20k resistor, 4k resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node B. The 4k resistor and Rload connect at node C, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of the 9V source connects to the 8k resistor at node D. The 8k resistor, 10k resistor, and non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node E. The low-potential end of the 9V source, 10k resistor, and Rload connect to GND at node F.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=9~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=8~k$\Omega$, -*] ++(right:2);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++ (right:2) --++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$] ++(left:2) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=20~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp.out)+(0,0)$);		
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2,0)$) to [R, l_=$R_{LOAD}$] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD} \amp= 4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= 39.1~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate <m>V_{OC}</m> and <m>I_{SC}</m> to calculate <m>R_{TH}</m>, which is the resistance used to obtain the maximum power transferred to the load. The circuit is a non-inverting op-amp. Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_P</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_P \amp= 9~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{8~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch4-noninvertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate the gain of the op-amp circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G \amp= 1+\frac{20~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{OC}</m> is equal to the output voltage of the circuit. (Because the output is an open-circuit, no current flows through the 4<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor, and it does not contribute to the circuit.) Use <xref ref="ch4-effectivegainopamp" /> to calculate this voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= GV_{P}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (5)(5~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 25~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The short-circuit current is equal to the current between the output of the op-amp and ground when the load resistor is shorted. The output voltage of the op-amp will be 5<nbsp />V and the other end of the 4<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor will be grounded. This leads to a short-circuit current of</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{SC} \amp= \frac{5~\textrm{V}}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.25~\textrm{mA}.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance can now be calculated. This is equal to the load resistance that should be used to maximize the power transferred to the load.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{25~\textrm{V}}{6.25~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="theMaximumPower" /> to calculate the maximum power transferred to the load.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= \frac{(25~\textrm{V})^2}{4(4~\textrm{k}\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{625~\textrm{V}^2}{16~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 39.1~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex9">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the circuit gain of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit49" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit49">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex9" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit49">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit containing two op-amps voltage source Vs, 1k resistor, 2k-a resistor, 2k-b resistor, 6k-a resistor, 6k-b resistor, and 3k resistor. The circuit has 7 nodes. The high-potential end of Vs connects to the 1k resistor at node A. The 1k resistor, 2k-a resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 1 connect at node B. The 2k-a resistor, 2k-b resistor, and output of op-amp 1 connect at node C. The 2k-b resistor, 6k-a resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 2 connect at node D. The 6k-a resistor, 6k-b resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node E, labeled Vout. The 3k resistor, 6k-b resistor, and non-inverting input of op-amp 1 connect at node F. The low-potential end of Vs, 3k resistor, and non-inverting input of op-amp 2 connect to GND at node G.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=$V_S$] ($(opamp.-)-(2,1.5)$) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [short] ++(down:1.5);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,1.5)$) to [R, l_=3~k$\Omega$] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw	(8,0.5) node[op amp] (opamp2) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,1.5)$) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$, *-] ($(opamp2.out)-(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.out)-(0,1.5)$) to [short, -*] (opamp2.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (opamp2.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp2.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp2.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,1.25)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp2.out)+(0.5,0)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p><m>G = 1.5</m></p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>This circuit doesn't fit any of the standard op-amp circuit templates (inverting, non-inverting, summing, or difference amplifier). Therefore, the assumptions of an op-amp circuit (no input current, virtual node) can be applied with any useful circuit laws and theorems to calculate these parameters.</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the non-inverting node of the left op-amp. The voltage at the non-inverting node will be referred to as <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{0-V_X}{3~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X-V_{OUT}}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>-V_X \amp= 0.5V_X - 0.5V_{OUT}</mrow>
							<mrow>-1.5V_X \amp= -0.5V_{OUT}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= \frac{1}{3}V_{OUT}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting node of the right op-amp. The output voltage of the left op-amp will be referred to as <m>V_Y</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_Y}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{0-V_{OUT}}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_Y \amp= -\frac{1}{3}V_{OUT}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting node of the left op-amp. Then, plug in the values of <m>V_X</m> and <m>V_Y</m> defined above, and solve for <m>V_{OUT}/V_S</m>, which is equal to <m>G</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S-V_X}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X-V_Y}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_S - V_X	 \amp= 0.5V_X - 0.5V_Y</mrow>
							<mrow>V_S - \frac{1}{3}V_{OUT} \amp= 0.5(\frac{1}{3}V_{OUT}) - 0.5(-\frac{1}{3}V_{OUT})</mrow>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= \frac{1}{3}V_{OUT} + \frac{1}{6}V_{OUT} + \frac{1}{6}V_{OUT}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= \frac{4}{6}V_{OUT}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{OUT}}{V_S} \amp= 1.5</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4dividedfeedbackex10">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the output voltage and source gains of the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit410" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit410">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4dividedfeedbackex10" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit410">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 2 op-amps, 0.5V source (V1), 1V source (V2), four 2k resistors, one 4k resistor, and two 3k resistors. The high-potential end of V1 connects to 2k-a at node A. The 2k-a resistor, 4k resistor, and 2k-b resistor connect to the inverting input of op-amp 1 at node B. The 4k resistor, 2k-d resistor, and output of op-amp 1 connect at node C. The 2k-d resistor, 3k-a resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 2 connect at node D. The 2k-b resistor, 3k-a resistor, 3k-b resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node E, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of V2 connects to the non-inverting input of op-amp 2 at node F. The 2k-c resistor, 3k-b resistor, and non-inverting input of op-amp 1 connect at node G. The low-potential end of both sources and 2k-c resistor connect to GND at node H.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,2.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=0.5~V~(V1)] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [short] ++(down:2.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,2.25)$) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$] ++(left:2) node[ground] {};
									\draw	(8,0.5) node[op amp] (opamp2) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.25)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,2.75)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,2.25)$) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, *-] ($(opamp2.out)-(0,2.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.out)-(0,2.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp2.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (opamp2.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp2.+) to [V, l^=1~V~(V2)] ++(left:3.75) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp2.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, -*] ($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,2.75)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp2.out)+(0.5,0)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>G_1 \amp= 3</mrow>
							<mrow>G_2 \amp= 2.5</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>This circuit doesn't fit any of the standard op-amp circuit templates (inverting, non-inverting, summing, or difference amplifier). Therefore, the assumptions of an op-amp circuit (no input current, virtual node) can be applied with any useful circuit laws and theorems to calculate these parameters. Because there is a connection between the input of the left op-amp and the output of the right op-amp, cascading op-amp properties cannot be used to calculate the overall circuit gain.</p>

					<p>This solution will use superposition to solve the circuit. Adding the two subcircuit values of <m>V_{OUT}</m> will yield the overall value of <m>V_{OUT}</m>, and each subcircuit can be used to calculate the individual source gain.</p>

					<p>Deactivate source <m>V_2</m> and draw the circuit diagram to calculate <m>G_1</m> and <m>V_{OUT,1}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit410sol1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with <m>V_2</m> deactivated.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit410sol1">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 2 op-amps, a 0.5V source (V1), four 2k resistors, two 3k resistors, and 4k resistor. The circuit has 7 nodes. The high-potential end of V1 connects to the 2k-a resistor at node A. The 2k-a resistor, 2k-b resistor, 4k resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 1 connect at node B. The 4k resistor, 2k-d resistor, and output of op-amp 1 connect at node C. The 2k-d resistor, 3k-a resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 2 connect at node D. The 2k-b resistor, 3k-a resistor, 3k-b resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node E, labeled Vout,1. The 2k-c resistor, 3k-b resistor, and non-inverting input to op-amp 1 connect at node F. The low-potential end of V1, 2k-c resistor, and non-inverting input of op-amp 2 connect to GND at node G.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,2.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=0.5~V~(V1)] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [short] ++(down:2.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,2.25)$) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$] ++(left:2) node[ground] {};
									\draw	(8,0.5) node[op amp] (opamp2) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.25)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,2.75)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,2.25)$) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, *-] ($(opamp2.out)-(0,2.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.out)-(0,2.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp2.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (opamp2.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT,1}$};
									\node[ground] at (opamp2.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp2.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, -*] ($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,2.75)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp2.out)+(0.5,0)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input of the left op-amp. This node voltage will be referred to as <m>V_X</m>. The left op-amp's output voltage will be referred to as <m>V_Y</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{0.5~\textrm{V}-V_X}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X-V_Y}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{V_X-V_{OUT,1}}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.25~\textrm{V} - 0.5V_X \amp= 0.25V_X - 0.25V_Y + 0.5V_X - 0.5V_{OUT,1}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.25~\textrm{V} \amp= 1.25V_X - 0.25V_Y - 0.5V_{OUT,1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the non-inverting input of the left op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{0-V_X}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X-V_{OUT,1}}{3~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>-0.5V_X \amp= 0.33V_X - 0.33V_{OUT,1}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 0.83V_X - 0.33V_{OUT,1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input of the right op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_Y}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{0-V_{OUT,1}}{3~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.5V_Y \amp= -0.33V_{OUT,1}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 0.5V_Y + 0.33V_{OUT,1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>V_Y</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>V_{OUT,1}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1.25 	\amp -0.25	\amp -0.5	 	\amp 0.25	\\
								0.83	\amp 0		\amp -0.33		\amp 0		\\
								0		\amp 0.5	\amp 0.33		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0	\amp 0.6\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0	\amp 1.0\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1	\amp 1.5
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{OUT,1}</m> is therefore equal to 1.5<nbsp />V. The source gain from source <m>V_1</m> can now be calculated.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G_1 \amp= \frac{1.5~\textrm{V}}{0.5~\textrm{V}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Deactivate source <m>V_1</m> and draw the circuit diagram to calculate <m>G_2</m> and <m>V_{OUT,2}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit410sol2">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with <m>V_1</m> deactivated.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit410sol2">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 2 op-amps, 1V source (V2), four 2k resistors, two 3k resistors, and 4k resistor. The circuit has 7 nodes. The 2k-a resistor, 2k-b resistor, 4k resistor, and inverting input to op-amp 1 connect at node A. The 4k resistor, 2k-d resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node B. The 2k-d resistor, 3k-a resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 2 connect at node C. The 2k-b resistor, 3k-a resistor, 3k-b resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node D, labeled Vout,2. The high-potential end of V2 connects to the non-inverting input of op-amp 2 at node E. The 2k-c resistor, 3k-b resistor, and non-inverting input to op-amp 1 connect at node F. The 2k-a resistor, 2k-c resistor, and low-potential end of V2 connect to GND at node G.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(0,2.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) node[ground] {} to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$, *-] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [short] ++(down:2.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,2.25)$) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$] ++(left:2) node[ground] {};
									\draw	(8,0.5) node[op amp] (opamp2) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.25)$) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,2.75)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(0,2.25)$) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, *-] ($(opamp2.out)-(0,2.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.out)-(0,2.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp2.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (opamp2.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT,2}$};
									\draw[]	(opamp2.+) to [V, l^=1~V~(V2)] ++(left:3.75) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short, *-] ($(opamp2.-)+(0,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, -*] ($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(2.88,2.75)$) to [short, -*] ($(opamp2.out)+(0.5,0)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input of the left op-amp. This node voltage will be referred to as <m>V_X</m>. The left op-amp's output voltage will be referred to as <m>V_Y</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{0-V_X}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X-V_Y}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{V_X-V_{OUT}}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>-0.5V_X \amp= 0.25V_X - 0.25V_Y + 0.5V_X - 0.5V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 1.25V_X - 0.25V_Y - 0.5V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the non-inverting input of the left op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{0-V_X}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{V_X-V_{OUT,2}}{3~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>-0.5V_X \amp= 0.33V_X - 0.33V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 0.83V_X - 0.33V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input of the right op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_Y-1~\textrm{V}}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= \frac{1~\textrm{V}-V_{OUT}}{3~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.5V_Y - 0.5~\textrm{V} \amp= 0.33~\textrm{V} - 0.33V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.83~\textrm{V} \amp= 0.5V_Y + 0.33V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>V_Y</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>V_{OUT,2}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1.25 	\amp -0.25	\amp -0.5	 	\amp 0		\\
								0.83	\amp 0		\amp -0.33		\amp 0		\\
								0		\amp 0.5	\amp 0.33		\amp 0.83
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0	\amp 1.0\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0	\amp 0.0\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1	\amp 2.5
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{OUT,2}</m> is therefore equal to 2.5<nbsp />V. The source gain from source <m>V_2</m> can now be calculated.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G_2 \amp= \frac{2.5~\textrm{V}}{1~\textrm{V}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.5</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= V_{OUT,1} + V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.5~\textrm{V} + 2.5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Op-Amp Output Current</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the current through and power consumed by the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; load resistor in the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit411" />. (Express this current as a positive value if it flows from <m>V_{OUT}</m> to ground, and negative if it flows from ground to <m>V_{OUT}</m>.) Then, calculate the current sourced by the op-amp. (Express this current as a positive value if the op-amp sources the current, and as a negative value if the op-amp sinks the current.)</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit411">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit411">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 8V source and 2k resistor. The circuit has 3 nodes. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node A. The inverting input of the op-amp, output of the op-amp, and 2k resistor connect at node B, labeled Vout. The low-potential end of the 8V source and 2k resistor connect to GND at node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=8~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.5) -| (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= 4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= 32~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_{SOURCE} \amp= 4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The op-amp circuit is a voltage follower. The output voltage is equal to <m>V_P</m>, which is 8<nbsp />V. Use Ohm's law to calculate the current through the load resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V}}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> to calculate the power consumed by the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= (4~\textrm{mA})^2(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (16~\textrm{mA}^2)(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 32~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>No current enters through the inverting node of the op-amp. Therefore, all of the current that flows through the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; load resistor is sourced from the op-amp.</p>
						
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise  xml:id="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the current through and power consumed by the 50<nbsp />&#8486; load resistor in the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit412" />. (Express this current as a positive value if it flows from <m>V_{OUT}</m> to ground, and negative if it flows from ground to <m>V_{OUT}</m>.) Then, calculate the current sourced by the op-amp. (Express this current as a positive value if the op-amp sources the current, and as a negative value if the op-amp sinks the current.)</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit412">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit412">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 1V source, 10ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor. The circuit has 4 nodes. The high-potential end of the 1V source connects to the 10ohm resistor at node A. The 10ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node B. The 20ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node C, labeled Vout. The low-potential end of the 1V source, non-inverting input of the op-amp, and 50ohm resistor connect to GND at node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=1~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, *-] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= -40~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= 80~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_{SOURCE} \amp= -140~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The op-amp circuit is an inverting op-amp. Apply <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> and <xref ref="ch4-effectivegainopamp" /> to calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= -(1~\textrm{V})\frac{20~\Omega}{10~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the current through the 50<nbsp />&#8486; load resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= \frac{-2~\textrm{V}}{0.05~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -40~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> to calculate the power consumed by the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= (-40~\textrm{mA})^2(0.05~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1600~\textrm{mA}^2)(0.05~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 80~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit diagram below is drawn with current directions.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit412sol1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with currents contributing to the op-amp output current shown.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit412sol1">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 1V source, 10ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor. The circuit has 4 nodes. The high-potential end of the 1V source connects to the 10ohm resistor at node A. The 10ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node B. The 20ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node C, labeled Vout. The low-potential end of the 1V source, non-inverting input of the op-amp, and 50ohm resistor connect to GND at node D. Current Ix flows through the 20ohm resistor from node C to node B. -40mA flows through the 50ohm resistor from node C to node D. Isource flows out of the output of the op-amp into node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [V, l_=1~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$] ++(right:3.5);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1.5,0)$) to [short, *-, i=$I_X$] ++(up:1.5) |- ($(opamp.-)+(3.5,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2,0)$) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, *-, i=--40~mA] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, i^=$I_{SOURCE}$] ++(right:1) --++ (right:2) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL at the op-amp output node to calculate <m>I_{SOURCE}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{SOURCE} \amp= I_X - 40~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-2~\textrm{V} - 0~\textrm{V}}{0.02~\textrm{k}\Omega} - 40~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -100~\textrm{mA} - 40~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -140~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise  xml:id="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the current through and power consumed by the 220<nbsp />&#8486; load resistor in the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit413" />. (Express this current as a positive value if it flows from <m>V_{OUT}</m> to ground, and negative if it flows from ground to <m>V_{OUT}</m>.) Then, calculate the current sourced by the op-amp. (Express this current as a positive value if the op-amp sources the current, and as a negative value if the op-amp sinks the current.)</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit413">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit413">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit containing a 0.6V source, 30ohm resistor, 40ohm resistor, and 220ohm resistor. The circuit has 4 nodes. The 30ohm resistor, 40ohm resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node A. The 40ohm resistor, 220ohm resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node B, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of the 0.6V source connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node C. The low-potential end of the 0.6V source and 220ohm resistor connect to GND at node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=0.6~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) node[ground] {} to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=220~$\Omega$, *-] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= 6.36~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= 8.9~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_{SOURCE} \amp= 26.4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The op-amp circuit is a non-inverting op-amp. Apply <xref ref="ch4-noninvertingopampgaineq" /> and <xref ref="ch4-effectivegainopamp" /> to calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= (0.6~\textrm{V})\left(1+\frac{40~\Omega}{30~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the current through the 220<nbsp />&#8486; load resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= \frac{1.6~\textrm{V}}{0.22~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.36~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> to calculate the power consumed by the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= (6.36~\textrm{mA})^2(0.22~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (40.5~\textrm{mA}^2)(0.22~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.9~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit diagram below is drawn with current directions.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit413sol1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with currents contributing to the op-amp output current shown.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit413sol1">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit containing a 0.6V source, 30ohm resistor, 40ohm resistor, and 220ohm resistor. The circuit has 4 nodes. The 30ohm resistor, 40ohm resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node A. The 40ohm resistor, 220ohm resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node B, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of the 0.6V source connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node C. The low-potential end of the 0.6V source and 220ohm resistor connect to GND at node D. Ix flows through the 40ohm resistor from node B to node A. 6.36mA of current flows through the 220ohm resistor from node B to node D. Isource flows out of the output of the op-amp into node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=0.6~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) node[ground] {} to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$] ++(right:3.5);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1.5,0)$) to [short, *-, i=$I_X$] ++(up:1.5) |- ($(opamp.-)+(3.5,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2,0)$) to [R, l_=220~$\Omega$, *-, i=6.36~mA] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, i^=$I_{SOURCE}$] ++(right:1) --++ (right:2) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL at the op-amp output node to calculate <m>I_{SOURCE}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{SOURCE} \amp= I_X + 6.36~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1.4~\textrm{V} - 0.6~\textrm{V}}{0.04~\textrm{k}\Omega} + 6.36~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{mA} + 6.36~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 26.4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise  xml:id="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>The LM118 op-amp chip can sink up to 65<nbsp />mA of current. Calculate the smallest load resistor that can be used in the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit414" />. Then, calculate the power consumed by that resistor.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit414">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit414">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with 3V source, 6V source, 500ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, 1k resistor, and Rload. The high-potential end of the 3V source connects to the 500ohm resistor at node A. The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node B. The 500ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, 1k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node C. The 1k resistor, Rload, and output of the op-amp connect at node D. The low-potential end of both voltage sources, non-inverting input of the op-amp, and Rload connect to GND at node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(3,0)$) to [V, l_=3~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(3,0)$) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] ++(right:2);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(1,2)$) to [V, l_=6~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(1,0)$) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$] ++(down:2);
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, -*] ++(left:1);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=$R_{LOAD}$, *-] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD} \amp= 1.24~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= 1.04~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The op-amp circuit is an inverting summing op-amp. Apply <xref ref="ch4-invertingsummingampeq" /> to calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= (-1~\textrm{k}\Omega)\left(\frac{3~\textrm{V}}{0.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}+\frac{6~\textrm{V}}{0.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (-1~\textrm{k}\Omega)(6~\textrm{mA}+30~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (-1~\textrm{k}\Omega)(36~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -36~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the current through the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; feedback resistor that flows into the output node of the op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{F} \amp= \frac{0~\textrm{V} - (-36~\textrm{V})}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 36~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The op-amp can only sink 65<nbsp />mA of current. Calculate the current that can flow into the output node of the op-amp through the load resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= 65~\textrm{mA} - 36~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 29~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the minimum load resistance that can be used in the circuit so as not to exceed 29<nbsp />mA.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD,MIN} \amp= \frac{0~\textrm{V} - (-36~\textrm{V})}{29~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{36~\textrm{V}}{29~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.24~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>


					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> to calculate the power consumed by the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= (29~\textrm{mA})^2(1.24~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (841~\textrm{mA}^2)(1.24~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1,040~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.04~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>The LM741 op-amp chip can source up to 25<nbsp />mA of current. Calculate the smallest load resistor that can be used in the circuit shown in <xref ref="examplecircuit415" />. Then, calculate the power consumed by that resistor.</p>

					<figure xml:id="examplecircuit415">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch4-opampoutputcurrent-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch4-examplecircuit415">
    						<shortdescription>Op-amp circuit with a 4V source, 1k resistor, 5k resistor, and Rload. The 1k resistor, 5k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node A. The 5k resistor, Rload, and output of the op-amp connect at node B, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of the 4V source connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node C. The low-potential end of the 4V source, 1k resistor, and Rload connect to GND at node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=4~V] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) node[ground] {} to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] (opamp.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$] ($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(2.38,1.25)$) to [short, -*] (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=$R_{LOAD}$, *-] ++(down:2) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short] ++(right:1) node[right] {$V_{OUT}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD} \amp= 1.14~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= 502~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The op-amp circuit is a non-inverting op-amp. Apply <xref ref="ch4-noninvertingopampgaineq" /> and <xref ref="ch4-effectivegainopamp" /> to calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= (4~\textrm{V})\left(1+\frac{5~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (4~\textrm{V})(6)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the current through the 5<nbsp />k&#8486; feedback resistor that flows out of the output node of the op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{F} \amp= \frac{24~\textrm{V} - 4~\textrm{V}}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The op-amp can only source 25<nbsp />mA of current. Calculate the current that can flow out of the output node of the op-amp through the load resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= 25~\textrm{mA} - 4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 21~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the minimum load resistance that can be used in the circuit so as not to exceed 21<nbsp />mA.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD,MIN} \amp= \frac{24~\textrm{V}-0~\textrm{V}}{21~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{24~\textrm{V}}{21~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.14~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> to calculate the power consumed by the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= (21~\textrm{mA})^2(1.14~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (441~\textrm{mA}^2)(1.14~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 502~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>
	</exercises>
</chapter>